---
import Article from "../../../layouts/Article.astro";
import KeyTakeaway from "../../../components/KeyTakeaway.astro";
import InfoBox from "../../../components/InfoBox.astro";
import QuickFact from "../../../components/QuickFact.astro";
---

<Article
  title="Is Duel of Meloch Worth It? An Honest Look"
  description="A duel game, a new faction and an eighth mat in one box. Who should buy it?"
  game="duel-of-meloch"
  gameTitle="Duel of Meloch"
  category="Buying Guide"
  product="scythe-duel-of-meloch-insert"
  publishDate="2026-08-09"
>
  <p>
    Duel of Meloch is doing two jobs at once, and how good a purchase it is depends
    almost entirely on which of those jobs you actually need. It's a standalone
    two-player duel, and it's a Scythe faction expansion. Very few buyers want both
    equally.
  </p>

  <KeyTakeaway answer="Yes, if you own Scythe and play mostly two-player — you get a purpose-built duel plus a new faction and the eighth mat. Less obviously if you game in groups of three or more, since the duel half won't get used." />

  <div class="my-8 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
    <QuickFact label="Players" value="2 only" icon="👥" />
    <QuickFact label="Time" value="~45 min" icon="⏱️" />
    <QuickFact label="Released" value="July 2026" icon="📅" />
    <QuickFact label="Standalone" value="Yes" icon="📦" />
  </div>

  <h2>The Verdict at a Glance</h2>

  <div class="my-6 grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
    <div class="p-5 bg-emerald-50 rounded-xl border border-emerald-200">
      <h4 class="font-heading font-semibold text-sm text-emerald-800 mb-3">✅ Buy It If...</h4>
      <ul class="text-sm text-emerald-700/70 space-y-2">
        <li>• You own Scythe and most of your gaming is two-player</li>
        <li>• You want a Scythe-universe game that fits a weeknight</li>
        <li>• A new faction and the eighth mat appeal on their own</li>
        <li>• You like sharp, contested, small-map games</li>
        <li>• You travel with games and want something compact</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="p-5 bg-red-50 rounded-xl border border-red-200">
      <h4 class="font-heading font-semibold text-sm text-red-800 mb-3">❌ Skip It If...</h4>
      <ul class="text-sm text-red-700/70 space-y-2">
        <li>• You almost always play with three or more people</li>
        <li>• What you love about Scythe is the scale and the long arc</li>
        <li>• You want a gentle introduction to the universe</li>
        <li>• You already own a two-player duel you're happy with</li>
        <li>• Asymmetric teaching burdens put your group off</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>What It Gets Right</h2>

  <div class="my-6 space-y-3">
    <div class="p-4 bg-white rounded-xl border border-bark/10">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-1">
        <span class="px-2 py-0.5 bg-emerald-100 text-emerald-700 text-[11px] font-bold rounded-full">STRENGTH</span>
        <h4 class="font-heading font-semibold text-sm text-bark">It solves a real Scythe problem</h4>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-bark/60">Scythe at two players has always been the weakest way to play it — a big map with too much room and a long arc built for more people. A purpose-designed 45-minute duel is a genuinely better answer than the compromise most Scythe owners have been living with.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="p-4 bg-white rounded-xl border border-bark/10">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-1">
        <span class="px-2 py-0.5 bg-emerald-100 text-emerald-700 text-[11px] font-bold rounded-full">STRENGTH</span>
        <h4 class="font-heading font-semibold text-sm text-bark">The Meloch card catch-up is elegant</h4>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-bark/60">A four-star race could snowball badly. Making every star you place hand your opponent a reward builds the rubber band into the win condition rather than bolting it on, and it keeps finishes close without feeling artificial.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="p-4 bg-white rounded-xl border border-bark/10">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-1">
        <span class="px-2 py-0.5 bg-emerald-100 text-emerald-700 text-[11px] font-bold rounded-full">STRENGTH</span>
        <h4 class="font-heading font-semibold text-sm text-bark">You're getting an expansion in the box</h4>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-bark/60">Constantinople and the Adventurous mat drop straight into normal 1–7 player Scythe. For an existing owner, a good chunk of the price is buying content for a game you already play — see <a href="/games/duel-of-meloch/constantinople-faction-guide/">the faction guide</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>What to Be Cautious About</h2>

  <div class="my-6 space-y-3">
    <div class="p-4 bg-white rounded-xl border border-bark/10">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-1">
        <span class="px-2 py-0.5 bg-amber-100 text-amber-700 text-[11px] font-bold rounded-full">CAUTION</span>
        <h4 class="font-heading font-semibold text-sm text-bark">Strictly two players</h4>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-bark/60">There's no scaling here. If your gaming is a group of four, the duel half of this box is dead weight and you're paying a full price for a faction and a mat.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="p-4 bg-white rounded-xl border border-bark/10">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-1">
        <span class="px-2 py-0.5 bg-amber-100 text-amber-700 text-[11px] font-bold rounded-full">CAUTION</span>
        <h4 class="font-heading font-semibold text-sm text-bark">Two systems to learn at once</h4>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-bark/60">Asymmetry between two different games is more interesting than asymmetry between two factions — and it's also a heavier teach. If neither player knows Scythe or Expeditions, expect the first session to be slower than 45 minutes suggests.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="p-4 bg-white rounded-xl border border-bark/10">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-1">
        <span class="px-2 py-0.5 bg-amber-100 text-amber-700 text-[11px] font-bold rounded-full">CAUTION</span>
        <h4 class="font-heading font-semibold text-sm text-bark">It's too new to know how it holds up</h4>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-bark/60">Released July 2026. Whether a ten-location map stays interesting across fifty plays, and whether Constantinople turns out to be balanced, are open questions. Anyone who tells you otherwise right now is guessing.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <InfoBox type="warning" title="Buying it purely as an expansion?">
    Be honest about how often Scythe hits your table. A new faction and the last
    player mat are a real addition if you play multiplayer Scythe regularly. If
    the big box comes out twice a year, you're paying for a duel you won't play to
    get content you'll barely use.
  </InfoBox>

  <h2>The Bottom Line</h2>

  <p>
    For a Scythe owner who games mostly as a pair, this is close to an
    easy yes — it fixes the weakest way to play Scythe and pays you a faction and
    a mat for the trouble.
  </p>

  <p>
    For everyone else the case narrows quickly. Group gamers get an expansion at
    full box price. Newcomers get a good but demanding first lesson in the
    universe. Both are defensible, neither is obvious.
  </p>

  <p>
    If you do buy it and intend to play it often, the overhead worth attacking is
    the box itself — a 45-minute game punishes slow setup far more than Scythe
    ever did. That's covered in
    <a href="/games/duel-of-meloch/duel-of-meloch-setup-and-teardown/">setup and teardown</a>.
  </p>
</Article>
